<Project>
  <!--
    All build outputs live OUTSIDE the repo under D:\Data\Laplace (see scripts/win/env.cmd).
    LAPLACE_BUILD_ROOT defaults on Windows so bare `dotnet build`/IDE builds land in the
    same tree as env.cmd-driven script builds.
  -->
  <!-- Shared compiler settings for every project under app/. -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <LAPLACE_BUILD_ROOT Condition="'$(LAPLACE_BUILD_ROOT)' == '' AND '$(OS)' == 'Windows_NT'">D:\Data\Laplace</LAPLACE_BUILD_ROOT>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(LAPLACE_BUILD_ROOT)' != ''">
    <BaseOutputPath>$(LAPLACE_BUILD_ROOT)\app\bin\$(MSBuildProjectName)\</BaseOutputPath>
    <BaseIntermediateOutputPath>$(LAPLACE_BUILD_ROOT)\app\obj\$(MSBuildProjectName)\</BaseIntermediateOutputPath>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- Never compile leftovers from a previous in-repo output layout. -->
    <DefaultItemExcludes>$(DefaultItemExcludes);obj/**;bin/**</DefaultItemExcludes>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <!--
    Tier=db RUNS by default. It was previously excluded here and in
    scripts/test-parallel.sh, so 66 useful PostgreSQL-backed tests executed nowhere,
    including the fold's bit-exact determinism proof. DB-tier tests must own/create
    their fixture state and may depend on PostgreSQL, but not on a particular seeded
    product corpus.

    Tier=live is intentionally different: it exercises the standing shared substrate
    and may require foundation/knowledge seeds or deposit durable product episodes.
    Those are explicit product acceptance probes, not unit/database-health tests, so a
    bare `dotnet test` does not run them accidentally during recreate/reseed work.

    Tier=perf also stays explicit because a throughput threshold on a busy self-hosted
    runner measures ambient machine load as much as code.

    Explicit filters still override the default:

      dotnet test /p:VSTestTestCaseFilter="Tier=db"    run DB-backed tests
      dotnet test /p:VSTestTestCaseFilter="Tier=live"  run seeded/shared product smokes
      dotnet test /p:VSTestTestCaseFilter="Tier=perf"  run performance tests intentionally
      dotnet test /p:VSTestTestCaseFilter=""           run absolutely everything
  -->
  <!-- IsTestProject is set by the test SDK, which imports AFTER this file, so it
       is empty here; key off the project name, which is known immediately. -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="$(MSBuildProjectName.EndsWith('.Tests')) AND '$(VSTestTestCaseFilter)' == ''">
    <VSTestTestCaseFilter>Tier!=perf&amp;Tier!=live</VSTestTestCaseFilter>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Outputs live outside the repo, so tests can no longer find the repo
         root by walking up from their own binary — stamp it instead. -->
    <AssemblyMetadata Include="LaplaceRepoRoot"
                      Value="$([System.IO.Path]::GetFullPath('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..'))" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <!--
    THE TEST MUST EXECUTE THE ARTIFACT BUILT FROM THIS TREE.

    /etc/ld.so.conf.d puts /opt/laplace/lib on the system loader path, so a bare
    `dotnet test` resolved liblaplace_core.so to the INSTALLED copy and never to
    build/engine/core. scripts/test-parallel.sh exports LD_LIBRARY_PATH and is
    correct; the bare invocation this file's own docs recommend for running a
    single test was not, and nothing said so.

    Measured 2026-08-24: LAPLACE_GLICKO2_NEUTRAL_MU_FP was changed 1500 -> 1400 and
    the library relinked, and NeutralMu_MatchesServerConstant stayed GREEN. It
    asserts a hard literal and would have caught the change; it was reading a copy
    installed 21 minutes earlier. Every native-backed parity assertion —
    Glicko2FoldParity, ConsensusKeysParity, CollapseIndexParity,
    QkPairsThresholdParity, RootIdNativeParityProbe — could pass against a stale
    installed library while the source defining the invariant was broken.

    Copying the built libraries beside the test binary fixes it for every
    invocation rather than for the ones that remember an environment variable:
    .NET probes NATIVE_DLL_SEARCH_DIRECTORIES, which includes the app base
    directory, BEFORE handing off to the OS loader — so an app-local copy wins
    over /opt/laplace/lib without touching a shared path.

    The self-hosted runner intentionally preserves build/app output trees between
    jobs and runs. Therefore timestamp-based PreserveNewest is not a correctness
    rule: a stale copied .so may have a newer mtime than a freshly relinked native
    artifact and survive into the next managed test. The payload is tiny relative
    to a test build; copy it unconditionally so artifact identity is content-driven,
    never mtime-driven.

    Ship the same closure with executable publishes as well. If integration fails
    after the shared engine is installed, restore-api must not start an old managed
    layout against a new native ABI. App-local native libraries keep the previous
    published payload coherent until the next complete publish.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(OS)' != 'Windows_NT'">
    <LaplaceNativeArtifact Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../build/engine/core/*.so*" />
    <LaplaceNativeArtifact Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../build/engine/dynamics/*.so*" />
    <LaplaceNativeArtifact Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../build/engine/synthesis/*.so*" />
    <None Include="@(LaplaceNativeArtifact)">
      <Link>%(Filename)%(Extension)</Link>
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>Always</CopyToOutputDirectory>
      <CopyToPublishDirectory>Always</CopyToPublishDirectory>
      <Visible>false</Visible>
    </None>
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
